digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "N10" [label="(10,0,0) More importantly, in these devices, the omnipresent nanogap between TMDs and transferred oxides denies physical contact with the channel materials and also suppresses the gate leakage currents, allowing metal oxides with moderate bandgaps as good dielectrics that’s somewhat counterintuitive."];
  "N11" [label="(11,0,0) In the context of all-in-one in-sensor computing in 2D devices, one will expect that one certain layer in heterostructures is a sensing layer, for example, sensitively responsible to external stimuli and effectively transferring this signal to neighboring layers, or even a multifunctional layer to reduce the layer number and simplify the structure."];
  "N13" [label="(13,0,0) When used as a component in 2D devices, the traditional dielectrics prepared by the gas-to-solid method are limited in material types and functions because of either the restricted gaseous precursors or the transferable prerequisite."];
  "N15" [label="(15,0,0) Merging two broad trajectories in separate fields, solution-processed metal oxides as dielectrics in 2D devices, remains an unexplored regime, and it can in principle offer more possibilities and candidates for advanced 2D electronics if the resulting metal oxides are compatible with 2D technology, for example, being able to be transferred and vdW integrated to form heterostructures."];
  "N27" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-rule: If all-in-one fusing creates technologies beyond von Neumann, then in-sensor/memory computing for pattern recognition is enabled."];
  "N29" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Currently there is all-in-one success (N3), and if beyond von Neumann then enabled, so in-sensor computing success."];
  "N3" [label="(3,0,0) Recently, all-in-one perceptual, memory and computational fusing in 2D devices have achieved tremendous success by creating technologies beyond von Neumann architecture, enabling in-sensor/memory computing for, e.g., image and acoustic pattern recognition and processing."];
  "N33" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-rule: If vdW surface of 2D semiconductors, then critical challenge integrating high-quality high-k dielectrics as in silicon."];
  "N35" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Currently there is vdW challenge (N4), and if vdW then challenge, so critical high-k integration challenge."];
  "N36" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-rule: If absence of dangling bonds on 2D surfaces, then poor nucleation in ALD for amorphous high-k (HfO2/Al2O3), leading to defects and doping."];
  "N38" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Currently there is absence causing poor nucleation (N5), and if absence then poor, so suboptimal interface."];
  "N4" [label="(4,0,0) The vdW surface of 2D semiconductors, however, also leads to one critical challenge: integrating high-quality, high-k dielectrics as silicon did."];
  "N42" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-rule: If epitaxially grown by gas-to-solid, then excellent dielectrics for back-gate."];
  "N44" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Currently there is CaF2 gas-to-solid (N7), and if gas-to-solid then excellent, so CaF2 excellent."];
  "N48" [label="(0,0,0) induction-common: Generally, transferred single-crystal high-k oxides (e.g. perovskites) can be incorporated into TMD devices with good properties."];
  "N5" [label="(5,0,0) For conventional amorphous high-k dielectrics, including hafnium oxide (HfO2) and aluminum oxide (Al2O3), the absence of dangling bonds on 2D surfaces results in poor nucleation during the direct deposition methods such as atomic layer deposition (ALD), leading to suboptimal interface quality characterized by increased defects and undesirable doping to TMDs layers."];
  "N50" [label="(0,0,0) induction-reasoning: From specific SrTiO3 transfer case (N9), and common transferred oxides work, so transfer allows high-quality dielectrics."];
  "N51" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-rule: If omnipresent nanogap between TMDs and transferred oxides, then denies contact and suppresses leakage, allowing moderate-bandgap oxides as good dielectrics."];
  "N53" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Currently there is nanogap (N10), and if nanogap then suppresses, so allows moderate bandgap oxides."];
