  "N56" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Currently there is in-sensor context (N11), and if in-sensor then expect multifunctional, so multifunctional layer expected."];
  "N60" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-rule: If traditional dielectrics by gas-to-solid, then limited in types/functions due to gaseous precursors or transferable prerequisite."];
  "N62" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Currently there is gas-to-solid limited (N13), and if gas then limited, so traditional limited."];
  "N69" [label="(15,2,0) abduction-knowledge: If resulting metal oxides compatible with 2D (transferable + vdW integrated), then offer more possibilities/candidates for advanced 2D electronics."];
  "N7" [label="(7,0,0) CaF2 epitaxially grown also by the gas-to-solid method has been demonstrated as excellent dielectrics for the back-gate configuration."];
  "N9" [label="(9,0,0) For instance, single-crystal perovskite strontium titanium oxide (SrTiO3), which holds a high static permittivity, can be first fabricated on sacrificial layers by pulsed laser deposition and then transferred as a freestanding flake to be incorporated into TMDs devices."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This study proposes solution-processed amorphous copper calcium titanate (CCTO) films as transferable, van-der-Waals-integrated functional layers for 2D transition-metal dichalcogenide devices, serving as high-k dielectrics for low-hysteresis, near-ideal-subthreshold FETs and as optically addressable layers enabling reconfigurable logic-in-sensor operation.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) A transferable, van-der-Waals-compatible, solution-processed oxide can address high-k dielectric integration needs in 2D transition-metal dichalcogenide devices while also enabling additional sensing or memory functions when the oxide has suitable optical/electronic properties."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) The paper identifies merging solution-processed metal oxides as dielectrics in 2D devices as an unexplored regime whose value depends on compatibility with 2D integration."];
